THE SUPERVISOR IS A DETACHED systemd USER SERVICE with an exact name: the script runs
  setsid systemd-run --user --wait --collect --unit=hoststep-fake1-<HHMMSS> -p Type=exec -p KillMode=mixed
    -p TimeoutStopSec=infinity -p MemoryMax=8G -p MemorySwapMax=0 -p "UnsetEnvironment=RR_CAMPAIGN_JOB RR_CAMPAIGN_FAILPOINT"
    -p StandardError=append:OUT/run.log -p StandardOutput=append:OUT/supervisor.stdout --working-directory=OUT
    --setenv=PATH=<the launching shell's PATH> --setenv=HOME=<HOME> [--setenv=TMPDIR=<TMPDIR if set>]
    -- bin/rr_campaign run --spec OUT/spec.json --plain          (dry-run.txt prints it in full)
  - the unit's main process IS the supervisor (systemd makes it its own session leader); its jobs start their own sessions
    and their own rr-<id>-* scopes;
  - the --wait client runs in its own session (output OUT/launcher.log) and reports systemd's exit status of the unit
    (P1 needs exit 0 AND systemd's "code=exited, status=0"; a death by signal is 128 + the signal, never 0);
  - follow: systemctl --user status hoststep-fake1-<HHMMSS>.service (exact name in OUT/supervisor.unit; unloaded when it
    ends: --collect); if the launching script ended early: REL=<dir>/bin OUT=<OUT> bash <dir>/host_step_fake1.sh --grade
    (follows the same unit by name, waits up to 30 s for the client's end report, then grades);
  - stop: <dir>/bin/rr_campaign control OUT/c stop (a request; the watcher's ABORT files exactly this), or
    systemctl --user stop hoststep-fake1-<HHMMSS>.service (SIGTERM to the supervisor = the same graceful stop, exit 143,
    P1 FAIL; systemd never escalates against the supervisor: TimeoutStopSec=infinity). KillMode=mixed: at a stop systemd
    sends SIGTERM to the supervisor only; once the supervisor has EXITED (a stop or a normal end) systemd sends SIGKILL to
    whatever is still in the SERVICE's own cgroup: only descendants of the supervisor that never moved into a job scope
    (its own systemctl helpers, a job's systemd-run client before its scope move). The jobs' scopes are separate units
    and are never touched by it; no foreign process can be in that cgroup.

THE TWO WORKLOADS (round 12; the same script, the same grader, P1-P8 as in RUNBOOK 1.7, P5 STRICT for both):
  (A) NON-FORKING, as host step 2: HELPERS unset (= 0): the fake jobs fork nothing.
  (B) HONESTLY FORKING: HELPERS=2: every rule job forks 2 compliant helpers inside its own scope (same session and group,
