KNOWN LIMIT L15-1 (RUNBOOK 2.10): a marked fork chain that forks and exits with no pause (0 us per hop), re-parented to
the supervisor, can stay unstoppable for the hunt (gate 15: never stopped in 105 s in a namespace without a scope): the
job stays Unverified, then `unproven_end` after 600 s, exit 8, the processes named; the exit is never held. On the host
the job's own scope ends it: the hunt's scope belt writes cgroup.kill to that scope after the guarded kill (a chain stays
in the scope of the job that forked it). The forking workload (B) has no such chain.

THE SAFE HOST SCOPE CHECK (round 11, gate14 item 5; round 12 m15-hc-stop): from this directory, on the host, outside any
job: `bash host_scope_check.sh --dry-run` (refusals and plan; nothing started), then `bash host_scope_check.sh --run`:
a NEW random unit name gate-scopecheck-<16 hex from /dev/urandom> each time (a name already loaded, or whose scope
directory exists, is refused); ONE scope of that name whose processes all live in their own private pid namespace; the
belt's record and live_members() checked read-only; a safety gate (every pid in that scope is its own unshare or inside
its private namespace, else ABORT without writing); ONE cgroup.kill of that scope; that exact unit stopped / reset-failed
if anything is left, and ONLY if this run created it (its own child was seen inside it); PASS only when every step held
and the unit is no longer loaded. Report: OUT/report.txt (path printed). bin/rr_scope_check is release-built from the
same commit; scope-check-refusal.txt shows it refusing inside a namespace (made by the freeze). It signals nothing else,
uses no pattern, and touches no other unit.

HOW TO READ P5: P5 stays STRICT: any unproven_end note FAILs it. When unproven_end notes exist, pass.txt carries an extra
line "P5-unproven INFO: N unproven_end note(s) (<attempts>): k of N cleared later (unverified_end_cleared of the same
attempt); signals not accounted for 0, POSSIBLY SENT 0, other P5 notes none: the strict P5 FAILs ONLY on these notes".
If that line says k == N, 0 unaccounted signals and "ONLY on these notes", the FAIL of P5 is a proof-completeness FAIL,
not a signal or containment one: the coordinator decides whether to accept it. Any other P5-unproven text: a real FAIL.
For workload (B) the expected P5 is a plain PASS: 0 unproven_end notes (rehearsal of this directory: see RUNBOOK).

